WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.377
OK, what are you doing?

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.800
It stops the suit from riding up.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.010
I've actually tried that.
Hairspray does not work.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.560
I use something much stronger.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.760
Hi, my name is Cheslie Kryst.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.680
I'm a former Miss USA, Miss USA 2019.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.710
Today, we're going to be
watching pageant scenes

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.618
from TV and film and
judging how real they are.

00:00:20.902 --> 00:00:23.750
Oh, my God! My tap costume's gone!

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.936
You heard me! Where is it?

00:00:25.936 --> 00:00:28.128
Get it off! I might even take seconds!

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:29.870
I've competed in, I don't know,

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.600
almost 20 pageants in my entire lifetime.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.600
I've literally never seen
something unfold that way.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.508
You may see some

00:00:37.508 --> 00:00:39.820
sort of underlying
cattiness between people,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.750
but not anything that would be different

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:42.950
from what you might see in, like,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.441
a regular workplace.

00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:46.980
Like, I've heard at one
international competition

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.480
somebody put gum on somebody's dress.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.670
You know the rules.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.660
All talent costumes have to be OK'd

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:53.960
by Gladys before the pageant.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.650
Her having to approve costumes,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.210
that is a real thing.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.647
They would have to see your swimsuit

00:00:58.647 --> 00:00:59.613
to make sure that, you know,

00:00:59.613 --> 00:01:02.120
your swimsuit is appropriate
for the competition.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.360
For Miss USA and for Miss Universe,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.660
obviously, those
competitions are televised.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.610
A specific person takes photos of us

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.110
in every single outfit we wear on stage

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.382
so that they can approve
it for television.

00:01:14.382 --> 00:01:16.032
My jacket.

00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:16.923
Take it! 'Cause, you know,

00:01:16.923 --> 00:01:19.290
I got my costume OK'd a month
ago, before the pageant.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.790
You could wear it! Come on!

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.943
One of the things that
people miss out on pageants

00:01:22.943 --> 00:01:25.238
is really how kind and generous

00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:27.470
most of the contestants truly are.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.093
I've actually been in a state competition

00:01:30.093 --> 00:01:31.507
where a woman signed up to compete

00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:32.870
and she didn't know how
to do her own makeup.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.378
And so there were,
like, four or five girls

00:01:34.378 --> 00:01:36.953
surrounding her literally
doing her makeup for her

00:01:36.953 --> 00:01:38.120
at this competition.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.900
I've seen girls, you know, give each other

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.170
shoes to wear or jewelry to wear.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.090
Yeah, I'd give it a 6.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.397
Generally, women who compete in pageants

00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:48.810
are so sweet and genuinely nice,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:49.942
and there are, like, a small fraction

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:51.883
every now and then you
run into that are catty.

00:01:53.309 --> 00:01:54.275
Gracie: Hello!

00:01:54.275 --> 00:01:55.108
Ah!

00:01:57.563 --> 00:01:59.210
Agent: Your pageant identity.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.020
"Miss Congeniality" is, like,
every pageant girl's movie.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.370
We quote this movie.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.542
This is obviously sort
of a souped-up version

00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:07.920
of what really happens,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.603
but in a lot of cases, when you win,

00:02:10.603 --> 00:02:13.940
you have a committee that
is there to support you.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.960
So, when I won my state pageant,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.290
we literally had, like, a retreat weekend.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.198
They brought in an interview coach,

00:02:20.198 --> 00:02:23.348
who gave us private interview sessions,

00:02:23.348 --> 00:02:25.440
went over our résumés with us.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.773
We had a makeup artist, who brought in

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:29.720
makeup that matched our skin tones

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.470
and gave us a makeup lesson.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.895
We had a self-defense coach,

00:02:32.895 --> 00:02:34.228
who came in and talked to us.

00:02:34.228 --> 00:02:36.983
So it felt a lot like this in one weekend.

00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:39.483
Hi! Um,

00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:41.300
well, my roommate's asleep.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.480
That absolutely is real.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.463
I've had a roommate.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.163
At my state competition, at Miss USA,

00:02:45.163 --> 00:02:47.330
and at Miss Universe, I've had a roommate.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.520
That's it.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.020
Excuse me.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.340
I am in the middle of
a REM cycle over here.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.708
Some days, you know, you had to be ready

00:02:53.708 --> 00:02:56.120
by maybe 6 or 7 o'clock in the morning.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.730
And if you think about hair
and makeup before then,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.096
you're up pretty early.

00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:01.063
You know, you try to get
as much rest as possible,

00:03:01.063 --> 00:03:01.947
but during pageant week,

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:04.010
sometimes it really just isn't possible.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.890
So, I think I got ...

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.572
five hours of sleep the
night before Miss USA.

00:03:10.455 --> 00:03:11.890
OK, what are you doing?

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.140
It stops the suit from riding up.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.900
Yeah, I think he's spraying
hairspray on her bottom.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.110
I've actually tried that.
Hairspray does not work.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.710
I use something much
stronger called Firm Grip,

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.410
which I think is the same product

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.920
that baseball players
spray in their mitts,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:27.970
so that they're more sticky

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.490
and they can catch balls easier.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.373
So it's very heavy-duty product,

00:03:31.373 --> 00:03:32.781
but there's other types of glues

00:03:32.781 --> 00:03:34.840
that we use to keep your bikini on.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.350
Otherwise, your swimsuit
may end up riding up

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.700
and you may have, like, a wedgie,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.820
and, you know, your butt cheeks may be

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.247
hanging out of your
swimsuit when you walk,

00:03:42.247 --> 00:03:43.767
and you don't want that, obviously.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.969
What, hemorrhoid ointment?

00:03:46.969 --> 00:03:48.980
It's for the little
baggies under your eyes.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.470
I haven't really tried.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:51.511
I think some people talk about

00:03:51.511 --> 00:03:53.227
using Vaseline on your teeth

00:03:53.227 --> 00:03:55.290
to make sure that your smile,

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.432
it stays on, sort of, when you're onstage.

00:03:57.432 --> 00:03:58.739
I've never done that.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:00.500
I think it might make it
more difficult to talk,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.940
or you might have product,
you know, in your teeth.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.448
And for those close-ups on television,

00:04:04.448 --> 00:04:06.500
you can actually see some of that.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:07.750
New Jersey.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.621
So, our sashes are usually

00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:13.671
taped to our shoulders onstage

00:04:13.671 --> 00:04:16.110
so that they don't fall
off of our shoulders.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.370
Our sarongs, when we compete in swimsuit,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.390
after we're done competing
and we're just standing,

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:21.428
I remember somebody came

00:04:21.428 --> 00:04:24.570
and literally tied the
sarong into my swimsuit,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:25.578
so that it wouldn't come off

00:04:25.578 --> 00:04:27.630
when they were making
the top 10 announcement.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.240
So, tons of sticky things on our bodies.

00:04:32.785 --> 00:04:33.630
Turn around.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.090
There's only women backstage.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.500
My dressers have always been women,

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.040
our chaperones are always women.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.640
But obviously pageants
have continued to evolve,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.330
and we do want to make
sure that it's a safe space

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.125
for people and that
people feel comfortable.

00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:48.320
Describe your perfect date.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.478
April 25th.

00:04:50.478 --> 00:04:54.061
Because it's not too hot, not too cold.

00:04:54.061 --> 00:04:55.179
This poor woman.

00:04:55.179 --> 00:04:57.200
[laughs]

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.970
I have to say, onstage question

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.180
is by far the most difficult

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.960
and stressful part of any competition.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.750
Partly because you're speaking onstage

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.030
in front of a lot of people.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:11.240
A lot of the times, it's the
last area of competition.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.250
You usually have a
really difficult question

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.420
about some controversial
or polarizing issue,

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.400
and usually you also have a time limit.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:19.525
If you've competed in a pageant

00:05:19.525 --> 00:05:20.910
and you haven't messed
up your onstage question,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.770
have you really competed in a pageant?

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.150
Let me have some of that.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.300
Oh, yeah.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.074
Guess we'll be needing some more pizza.

00:05:27.074 --> 00:05:28.970
In the competitions I've been in,

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.240
they've typically provided food.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.530
Especially if you're there for two weeks,

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.890
obviously we need food.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.680
And usually it's really healthy food.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.965
A lot of women are generally
eating really healthy

00:05:38.965 --> 00:05:39.931
when you're at a competition,

00:05:39.931 --> 00:05:40.956
'cause you're going to compete in swimsuit

00:05:40.956 --> 00:05:43.110
and you want to make
sure that whatever diet

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.962
or exercise you've put into your prep

00:05:44.962 --> 00:05:47.290
is still there when you're onstage.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.020
I would give it, like, an 8 1/2 or 9.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.145
And I like that, at the end of the day,

00:05:52.145 --> 00:05:55.267
"Miss Congeniality" portrayed women in --

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:57.550
you know, a lot of these
women were really nice,

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.890
they were real, they were imperfect.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.996
And that's actually what happened.

00:06:01.996 --> 00:06:03.287
Elbow, elbow,

00:06:03.287 --> 00:06:04.695
wrist, wrist, wrist.

00:06:04.695 --> 00:06:06.270
Elbow, elbow,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.586
wrist, wrist, wrist.

00:06:07.586 --> 00:06:10.570
There's a lot of coaching that
comes along with pageantry.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:11.880
I love the little wave, though.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.021
There's actually a thing, it's like,

00:06:13.021 --> 00:06:14.554
elbow, elbow, wrist, wrist,

00:06:14.554 --> 00:06:16.590
touch your pearls, blow a kiss.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.530
That's sort of like the
sort of pageant wave.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:19.560
We don't wave like that anymore.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.620
We just wave like regular people.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.220
Either you, like, do one of these

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.580
or wave like this,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.410
or you give, like, the big,

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:26.751
the big wave when you win.

00:06:26.751 --> 00:06:27.692
This is the big wave,

00:06:27.692 --> 00:06:29.330
where you've got your hand
all the way up in the air.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:30.412
That's a thing.

00:06:30.412 --> 00:06:33.437
Turn your body slowly.

00:06:33.437 --> 00:06:35.040
Very good, eye contact, yes.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.870
There are certain poses that
are specific to pageantry.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.680
Can I show you some of them?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.260
So, there's one pose that a lot --

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.880
here, I'll go to my
white space. There we go.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.630
That a lot of girls like
to do in evening gown,

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:48.600
where you pose like this

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.480
and then you put a hand, like, right here

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.970
on your thigh/leg area.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:54.810
And so it's supposed to be like this.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.570
A lot of people do, like, a model T,

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.690
where you've got, like, your foot,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.720
your front foot like this
and your back foot like this,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.060
and it's supposed to give you
this more hourglass shape.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.350
A lot of girls nowadays do,
like, the legs-apart pose.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:07.183
So, you're standing,

00:07:07.183 --> 00:07:08.690
I don't know if you can
see my legs, but, like,

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.080
you know, you've got
your legs out like this.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.610
One leg would be kind
of in front of the other

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:13.627
so that you look longer,

00:07:13.627 --> 00:07:15.485
your legs look leaner and everything.

00:07:15.485 --> 00:07:17.720
All of these are very
specific to pageantry.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.211
So, like, the kiddie pageants,

00:07:19.211 --> 00:07:21.677
like, you know, the
"Toddlers &amp; Tiaras" pageants,

00:07:21.677 --> 00:07:23.600
that kind of posing is very different

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.120
from what you would do at Miss USA.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.428
Oh, yeah. I would give this a 5.

00:07:26.428 --> 00:07:27.480
Because I can't say there was a whole lot

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.680
that I saw in these clips
that actually happens

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.343
the way that it's depicted here.

00:07:33.338 --> 00:07:37.740
Vicki: And Stillwater,
and you are Miss Tulsa.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.480
Somebody from her team is photoshopping

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.260
one of her pictures. That happens too.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.810
There's been a former Miss USA

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.159
who talked about, somebody from her team

00:07:45.159 --> 00:07:47.007
had photoshopped one of her photos

00:07:47.007 --> 00:07:49.420
and made her look really
skinny and really tall.

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.510
So she was at Miss
Universe, and, you know,

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:52.760
she's got this photoshopped picture.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.020
She looks really good.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.847
And then she saw some other pictures

00:07:55.847 --> 00:07:59.470
that some of the on-location
photographers had taken

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.040
that obviously weren't photoshopped,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.680
and she said that she felt
bad because she was like,

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:05.720
"Did I gain weight since I've been here?"

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.027
And she talks to her old team member,

00:08:07.027 --> 00:08:09.130
and they were like, "Well,
I photoshopped your photo."

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:10.210
And she's like,

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.090
"I wish I would have at least known that.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.470
Or maybe I wish you
wouldn't have done that,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.872
so that I at least had a real

00:08:15.872 --> 00:08:17.763
perspective on how my own body looks."

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:18.746
But it definitely happens.

00:08:18.746 --> 00:08:21.100
I hope people know that,
you know, we're not perfect.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.270
Obviously we want to put
our best foot forward,

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.180
but just know, you know,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.980
photoshopping and filtering happens.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.340
There's tons of people backstage,

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.860
or it can be at least just
as hectic as it looks here.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.554
There are hair and makeup people

00:08:37.554 --> 00:08:38.961
who are going around and doing touch-ups,

00:08:38.961 --> 00:08:39.990
there's hairspray all over,

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:42.568
there are people adjusting your costume

00:08:42.568 --> 00:08:45.680
or your gown or your swimsuit,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.530
there are members of the
production team back there

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.300
that are counting down on their radios.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.730
I just try to focus on the task at hand

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:55.270
and compartmentalize what's going on.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.000
Think about, where am I standing?

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.050
Where do I go after that?

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.900
If they call my name,
where do I step to next?

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.550
Yeah, I'd give this, like, a strong 8.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.568
And five, six, seven, eight!

00:09:09.998 --> 00:09:12.810
The difficulty of the dances
just varies by the pageant.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.987
Low key, we had an audition.

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:16.530
They don't call it audition,

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:17.720
but low key it kind of is.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.412
What the choreographers did for us

00:09:20.412 --> 00:09:21.750
is they taught all of us,

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.110
I think it was maybe two or three

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.300
eight counts of a dance,

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.930
and then they lined us up
in rows of six or whatever

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:28.480
and made us do the dance,

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.170
you know, sort of one row by one.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.173
And so some of the more
difficult dance moves

00:09:32.173 --> 00:09:34.160
they gave to people who
could pick it up quickly,

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.180
and some of the maybe walking patterns

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.140
they gave to people who
were less coordinated.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.002
And it has, like, no effect on who wins.

00:09:42.002 --> 00:09:44.520
You lied to me? You just lied?

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.499
You lied straight to my face?

00:09:46.499 --> 00:09:47.910
Is this who you are?

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.958
I've never seen a parent
randomly interrupt

00:09:50.958 --> 00:09:53.220
the middle of a pageant and come backstage

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.430
and try to take somebody home.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.510
I think that would be
way out of the ordinary.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:57.940
Not saying that it's never happened;

00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:59.702
I've just personally never seen it happen.

00:09:59.702 --> 00:10:01.250
And we have security backstage,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:02.772
and so you're not going to be able to just

00:10:02.772 --> 00:10:04.305
bust in the backstage area.

00:10:04.305 --> 00:10:05.813
But as a teen, yeah, you would need

00:10:05.813 --> 00:10:08.230
some level of parental consent.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:09.674
Pageants can be pretty costly,

00:10:09.674 --> 00:10:11.085
and so as a teen, if you don't have a job,

00:10:11.085 --> 00:10:12.605
you're going to need your parents' help,

00:10:12.605 --> 00:10:15.157
either to pay for your entry fees

00:10:15.157 --> 00:10:18.070
or to pay for your makeup, your wardrobe.

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:21.320
Now, as far as, like, having
family members not know,

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.460
that could definitely happen.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.070
I remember when I competed my final year

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.360
at Miss North Carolina USA,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.590
I didn't know if I would win.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:30.880
And I gotta tell you,

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.260
there is nothing that
puts more pressure on you

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.020
than knowing that your
friends and family have

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:41.210
driven or flown from far
away to watch you compete,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.690
have paid for hotel rooms,
have paid for tickets,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:44.656
are out there rooting for you,

00:10:44.656 --> 00:10:45.880
and then have you not win.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.713
It's terrible.

00:10:46.713 --> 00:10:48.900
For this one, I would give it a 7.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:50.933
The parent coming backstage is,

00:10:50.933 --> 00:10:52.710
it's not super realistic.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:55.600
Here we have Leslie's custom scorecard,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.850
with categories such as:

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.275
presentation, intelligence,

00:11:00.275 --> 00:11:01.983
knowledge of herstory.

00:11:01.983 --> 00:11:03.210
So, the judging criteria

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.800
for every competition is different.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.141
Some judges have scores

00:11:09.141 --> 00:11:10.349
that they'll put down

00:11:10.349 --> 00:11:14.460
either in swimsuit or
talent or onstage question.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:17.400
You have a score from, like, 5 to 10.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.550
I've actually judged a state competition

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.440
in the Miss Universe Organization,

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.718
and I really liked that we
didn't assign people numbers.

00:11:25.718 --> 00:11:28.000
So for swimsuit, for example,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.270
I wouldn't give someone a 10 out of 10.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.730
You would just make selections.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:35.410
If only 15 contestants get to advance

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:36.680
in the swimsuit competition,

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.790
you would watch everybody compete, all 40,

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:40.460
in the preliminary competition,

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.505
and then you would literally
just select 15 people

00:11:43.505 --> 00:11:45.457
that you thought should
advance in swimsuit.

00:11:45.457 --> 00:11:48.888
And I volunteer at the
children's hospital.

00:11:48.888 --> 00:11:50.141
[clapping]

00:11:50.141 --> 00:11:51.970
[upbeat dance music]

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.400
My girl Trish is talented!

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.180
Unless a judge makes, you know,

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.571
really inappropriate
comments or something,

00:11:58.571 --> 00:12:01.008
I don't see why they would kick them off

00:12:01.008 --> 00:12:02.950
just for not being a good judge.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.590
What makes a good judge or a bad judge?

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.960
I mean, you know, I think, like I said,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.297
it's really subjective,

00:12:08.297 --> 00:12:11.573
and what somebody may
appreciate in one contestant

00:12:11.573 --> 00:12:13.810
somebody may not be able to stand.

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:15.905
We don't get cash? This is for a fence?

00:12:15.905 --> 00:12:17.190
Well, it won't cover a whole fence,

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:19.002
but it will defray the cost considerably.

00:12:19.002 --> 00:12:21.840
In a system like the Miss
America Organization,

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.720
no matter what level you're competing at,

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.890
you get some sort of scholarship.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.550
Miss America gets, like,
a $50,000 scholarship.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.330
And Miss America is a salaried employee.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:33.913
So, you also get, you know,

00:12:33.913 --> 00:12:37.229
additional prizes and
stuff on top of that.

00:12:37.229 --> 00:12:38.828
Miss USA and Miss Universe,

00:12:38.828 --> 00:12:41.278
we are also salaried employees

00:12:41.278 --> 00:12:43.003
of the Miss Universe Organization.

00:12:43.003 --> 00:12:44.590
We also have housing.

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:47.500
We lived in a high-rise
in Manhattan, in New York,

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.270
for the the term that
we were titleholders.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.008
This one's kind of low, yes.

00:12:53.008 --> 00:12:54.216
I would rank this one pretty low,

00:12:54.216 --> 00:12:57.900
maybe, like, a 3 out of 10.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.680
I did like how they depicted
one of the competitors.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:01.692
I mean, she said that she, you know,

00:13:01.692 --> 00:13:04.207
she talked about her volunteerism,
that she was a student.

00:13:07.142 --> 00:13:09.892
[crowd protests]

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.807
So, this particular scene was 1970s,

00:13:14.807 --> 00:13:15.956
the protesters were part of

00:13:15.956 --> 00:13:17.790
the Women's Liberation Movement,

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:19.210
and this was at Miss World.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:20.472
If something like that happened now,

00:13:20.472 --> 00:13:22.297
I would assume that security

00:13:22.297 --> 00:13:23.960
would probably remove those people.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:25.850
Obviously, you know, voicing your opinion

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:29.740
and protesting is an important exercise,

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.610
but we also have a competition going on.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:34.012
We want the women who
are given an opportunity

00:13:34.012 --> 00:13:35.880
to make a change and make a difference.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.800
But we have had something
similar to this happen.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:40.510
A different kind of protest.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:44.890
During the election cycle for 2016,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:46.332
former President Donald Trump

00:13:46.332 --> 00:13:48.753
then owned the Miss Universe Organization.

00:13:48.753 --> 00:13:50.553
And you may remember that he made some

00:13:50.553 --> 00:13:52.570
awful, disparaging statements.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.720
Was talking about immigrants
crossing the border

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.290
and just was disparaging them.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.340
The network that owned the competition

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:03.060
I think decided not to air
the competition on television.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:04.880
The celebrity judges that were lined up

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:07.100
for the competition pulled out.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.530
I think the host pulled
out, the talent pulled out,

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:10.890
and rightfully so, right?

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:12.000
We have to say that

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:13.690
we don't stand for these kind of comments.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.400
I don't stand for those kinds of comments,

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.440
and nobody wants to be
associated with that.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.390
So, you know, on that end,
that was an important move.

00:14:19.390 --> 00:14:20.670
But on the other end,

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:22.900
you have these women who are titleholders

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:24.760
who also don't stand for that.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.060
And these women have prepared for a year,

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.230
and now we're, like, out of a competition

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:31.004
because this man decided to
make these awful statements.

00:14:31.004 --> 00:14:34.850
[crowd protesting]

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.190
There were similar protests

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:37.838
when Miss America and Miss USA

00:14:37.838 --> 00:14:40.430
were happening in the States.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.621
And there were many times when

00:14:41.621 --> 00:14:42.929
women would talk about pageants

00:14:42.929 --> 00:14:43.890
and say that they, you know,

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.295
weren't appropriate for the times

00:14:45.295 --> 00:14:47.440
or that they were disparaging
or objectifying to women.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:48.823
Obviously I don't believe that,

00:14:48.823 --> 00:14:50.798
but I think it's important that women

00:14:50.798 --> 00:14:52.860
and people in general voice their opinions

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:54.980
and try to push for progress
the way that they see fit.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.513
There are people who believe that pageants

00:14:57.513 --> 00:14:59.470
are just about showing
up and being pretty.

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:01.090
I've actually had someone say that to me

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:02.580
when I was a state titleholder.

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:04.750
I was standing backstage
at an awards show,

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.010
and this woman walked
up to me, and she said,

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:08.210
"It must be so hard just
showing up and being pretty."

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:12.280
Being Miss USA opened up so
many new opportunities for me

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:13.470
and gave me the opportunity to

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.500
speak about issues that were
incredibly important to me,

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.666
like Black Lives Matter,

00:15:17.666 --> 00:15:19.710
the resurgence of the
movement last summer.

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.878
I talked about immigration reform.

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:23.959
Is Miss Grenada.

00:15:23.959 --> 00:15:27.330
[crowd gasps]

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.020
Your mind goes totally
blank, or at least mine did.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:32.430
And I remember forgetting
where the cameras were.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.178
So, you're supposed to
look out into the stage

00:15:34.178 --> 00:15:35.470
so people can get your reaction.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:37.720
Instead, I'm holding hands with

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:39.610
my first runner-up,
who is Miss New Mexico,

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:41.940
and I literally just
collapsed into her arms,

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.570
and I'm sort of bending down like this.

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:44.970
And because I was doing that,

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.190
there are no reaction shots.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:47.910
Literally, all you're looking
at is the side of my head.

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:49.480
This is me when I win. [laughs]

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:53.013
So if you win, make sure
you look at the camera.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:03.610
I certainly knew about
the landmark titleholders

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:05.210
in the Miss Universe Organization.

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:06.468
Like, for example, Carole Gist,

00:16:06.468 --> 00:16:08.420
she was Miss USA 1990.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:12.190
She was the first Black Miss USA --

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:14.943
which is crazy to think
that in the '70s, you know,

00:16:14.943 --> 00:16:17.930
we already had a woman of
color who had won Miss World.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:20.038
So I feel like Miss
World and Miss Universe

00:16:20.038 --> 00:16:22.597
were obviously far more advanced

00:16:22.597 --> 00:16:24.610
when it came to racial diversity

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:27.530
than some of the
competitions in the States.

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.410
Even today, we're still dealing
with some of those issues.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:33.570
We're still dealing with
colorism in competitions,

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.110
we're still dealing with women feeling

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:37.510
like we have to do
code-switching in competitions

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:40.012
rather than just being able
to be our authentic selves.

00:16:40.012 --> 00:16:42.110
I would give it, like, an 8, for the time.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:42.943
Yeah.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:49.000
I've seen some pretty interesting talents.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.390
I can't say that I've seen
some very dangerous ones.

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.170
I think there's been, like,

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.860
luggage packing, years ago.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.190
The current Miss America,

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:59.870
I think she did a science
experiment for her talent.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.220
At Miss North Carolina and Miss America,

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.350
that's sort of the system
that still has talent.

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:07.330
There are always tons of tap dancers.

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:10.290
They are always tons
of musicians, singers,

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:12.048
oh, my -- so many singers.

00:17:12.048 --> 00:17:13.998
You would almost never see somebody

00:17:13.998 --> 00:17:16.690
doing the same song at one competition,

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:17.831
especially when you get to

00:17:17.831 --> 00:17:19.472
the state and the national level.

00:17:20.652 --> 00:17:23.810
[audience member mouthing answers]

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:27.160
Which is all about meeting and exceeding.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.850
Generally, you cannot see.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:32.300
I've never been able to see
into the audience that well.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:33.643
But as far as, like, being able

00:17:33.643 --> 00:17:35.793
to communicate messages back and forth,

00:17:35.793 --> 00:17:37.509
I don't think that that would be possible.

00:17:37.509 --> 00:17:38.480
Especially because, you know,

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.071
onstage question is usually timed,

00:17:40.071 --> 00:17:41.354
and you just don't have time to sit there

00:17:41.354 --> 00:17:43.860
and watch somebody, read their lips.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.650
I can't say that anything from this

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:47.650
is very realistic other than, you know,

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.411
a woman wearing a pretty dress onstage.

00:17:49.411 --> 00:17:51.343
I'm going to give this one a 2 out of 10.

